---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import site from '../data/site.json';
import { allQuestions, allPapers, FIRST_SERIES } from '../lib/data';
---
<Base title="About this site" context="about" description="Who runs lawaleveltutor.com and where the questions, mark schemes and ratings come from.">
  <h1>About this site</h1>
  <p>lawaleveltutor.com is run by {site.company}, an A-level tutoring business with a small team of specialist tutors. The site exists to give OCR, AQA and Cambridge International Law students the past-paper material they actually use, sorted the way they revise, free and without sign-up; and to introduce students who want more help to a tutor who has taught both the OCR and AQA specifications.</p>
  <h2>Where the material comes from</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Questions, mark schemes and examiner comments</strong> are from OCR's own published papers for every Law series since {FIRST_SERIES} (H418 A level and H018 AS), with OCR's sample assessment papers: {allPapers.length} papers and {allQuestions.length} questions. Each question has been tagged to the specification content it assesses, its assessment objectives and its question type. The nature of law essays that OCR prints on both Paper 3 and Paper 4 are counted once.</li>
    <li><strong>The AQA section</strong> (<a href="/aqa/">AQA A-level Law 7162</a>) covers every paper AQA has published: June 2022 to June 2025, the November 2020 and 2021 series and the specimen papers, for Papers 1, 2, 3A and 3B, with the mark schemes and, for the June papers, the examiners' reports. Difficulty ratings for its multiple-choice questions come from the share of students AQA's report says chose the right answer.</li>
    <li><strong>The Cambridge section</strong> (<a href="/cambridge/">Cambridge International AS &amp; A Level Law 9084</a>) covers every June and November paper from 2023, when the current syllabus was first examined, in every time-zone variant, plus Cambridge's specimen papers, with the mark schemes and examiners' reports. Most of the copies used are Cambridge's original PDFs taken from past-paper archives online, and every page credits Cambridge. Cambridge questions are not given difficulty ratings.</li>
    <li><strong>Difficulty ratings</strong> (easy, medium, hard) are our reading of what the examiners' report says about how candidates found each question. OCR's Law reports do not print average marks, so treat the ratings as a guide. Sample papers have no report and are rated by comparison.</li>
    <li><strong>Topic tags</strong> were made by the site and are being checked by our tutor; tell us if one looks wrong.</li>
    <li><strong>Key-term cards</strong> are original, written for this site; nothing derived from OCR's material is generated automatically.</li>
    <li><strong>Grade boundaries and exam dates</strong> are the boards' published figures; the pages say where each was taken from.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Who writes it</h2>
  <p>The tutoring is by Sue, a qualified solicitor who has taught A-level Law for 25 years; see the <a href="/tutors/">tutor page</a>. The site is built and maintained by the same small team that runs <a href="https://www.alevelbiologytutor.com" rel="noopener">alevelbiologytutor.com</a>, <a href="https://www.mathsaleveltutor.com" rel="noopener">mathsaleveltutor.com</a>, <a href="https://www.alevelpsychologytutor.com" rel="noopener">alevelpsychologytutor.com</a>, <a href="https://www.alevelsociologytutor.com" rel="noopener">alevelsociologytutor.com</a> and <a href="https://www.economicsaleveltutor.com" rel="noopener">economicsaleveltutor.com</a>.</p>
  <h2>Mistakes</h2>
  <p>If you find one, tell us on the <a href="/report/">report a mistake</a> page; corrections usually go live the same week.</p>
</Base>
